X10?  UWS?  Really-primitive-crap-10-years-behind-the-MAC?

I first tried a Lisa when in the PRO300 engineering group.  It took me 15 
minutes to figure out that you didn't run an application, you tore off a 
sheet of paper (and object) and then used tools to operate on it.  It was 
light years ahead of anything we had - even in B&W on a tiny screen.

My daughter at college recently switched to a Mac powerbook from a Windows 
laptop - I have to say they are still light years ahead of the pack in user 
interface technology.

MS windows started out as crude, non-ovelapping windows.  But it follows a 
pattern that MS has done from the beginning - "good enough, quick enough, 
cheap enough"... Oh - and leave enough crumbs on the table for other 
companies to make lots of money selling PC HW and applications.  The 
evolution of the Windows UI is a herky-jerky surge forward every few years - 
at the cost of making people re-learn a lot of things.

Motif was, well - not the original plan for X11.  The plan was to get MS to 
license Windows and implement it on top of X11 (which would then be the 
low-level, network transparent HW interface).  So each company went off with 
their GUI experts and created their own UI's and program interfaces.  Motif 
was the compromise blending of a number of these individual efforts into a 
common UI and programming interface.

Motif is essentially frozen in time - it can't move forward much because 
companies don't want to re-invest in rewriting their applications or 
retraining their users.  New UI work outside of Windows and Mac is in the 
Linux space.  Eventually they will figure out that X11 needs to be done away 
with - except for some compatability mode.  Or maybe not.





