Rich Alderson <news@alderson.users.panix.com> writes:

> Al Kossow <aek@spies.com> writes:

> I was the last Tops-20 systems programmer at LOTS, 1-Oct-1984 to 1-Nov-1991,
> so trust me to have this correct.

This was not for Al's benefit, because I know he knows this.

I also forgot to explain the name "Low Overhead Timesharing System".

At the time that LOTS was started, student computing at Stanford was like that
most other places:  Punch your cards, hand them in to the clerks at the Big IBM
Iron Data Center (which underwent several name changes over the next 15 years,
but never changed), wait some longish period of time, pick up your printout and
deck, and repeat.

Ralph Gorin's idea was that timesharing would be good for students the same way
it was good for professionals:  Faster turnaround, incremental experimentation
with programs, and all that.  As a graduate student, he didn't have much clout,
so he convinced John McCarthy of the goodness of this vision, and John got some
money for the project, and they bought a DEC-20 (both being great fans of the
hardware architecture).  And here's where the low overhead comes in.

There were 3.5FTE:  The part-time Director (originally John McCarthy, later
Ralph Gorin), the System Manager (originally Ralph), the System Programmer (I
forget who the first one was), and the Secretary/Administrative Assistant (who
was Queenette Baur from day 1 until she left about 1990).  All other functions
of this thriving data center were performed by students:  About half a dozen
who got paid for 10 hours a week, and dozens and dozens of volunteers.

Not low *system* overhead--nope, straight Tops-20 with load averages in the
high double digits.  Low *administrative* overhead, so that it didn't cost the
University all that much money to provide better computing facilities to more
students.

And *that* was LOTS.

